Again, this one worked for the test case provided. I had a doubt regarding the expected output and after looking into a few data sets, I was quite confident in my understanding of the problem.

Try factorial

Imagine we have two binary numbers, 0011 and 1100.  These are 15 and 12 in base 10 where there is a 1 at the second digit from the decimal point (so we call them 15 and 12)

What happens when we multiply these together?

class Game
{
public static void main (String[] args)
{
System.out.println(15*12);
}
}

The result will be: 180. In base 10, 180 is 0254.

As you can see in the number, the 2s and 4s are in the 10's place (second decimal point).

We can use this as a method to quickly find the multiples of larger numbers. For instance, if we wanted to find the multiples of 8. Multiplying 8 by every number from 1 to n is equivalent to multiplying 2 to the power of 4 by numbers in the same range.

This can be optimized further if we want to find multiples of 24:

24 * 15 * 12 = 24 * 2 * 16 * 3 * 4

= 252 * 96

